for the past few years Cadillac has been  working hard to change it has moved its  headquarters to New York City sponsored  fashion shows and flown the press and  VIPs to product lunches in Dubai more  importantly and it started making cars  like the CTSV which are incredibly fast  and powerful on the road the brand is  trying to shake off that fuddy-duddy  image the thing is those old Cadillacs  were rad the Fleetwood the Calais and  the series 60 and 63 lines were full of  style they meant something and when I  was in Detroit recently I went for a  drive down Cadillacs memory lane  that's when I met this 1967 Cadillac  DeVille it's a dream boat painted in  Persian ivory with snow-white seats on  the inside this is the signature sedan  that caddy made from 1946 to 2006 it  takes its name from the French term  Deville or of the town so as you might  expect this is a car for cruising around  town when it was introduced it was the  largest car in the model range at the  time checking in at more than eighteen  feet from tip to trunk this is  definitely the longest car I've driven  but it feels like you're on a cloud you  just float along kind of like a figure  skater on ice it's made totally out of  metal sculpted into boat like elegance  and festooned with luxury the front  grille looks like a silver rack set with  diamonds at each end in 1967 the car had  an eight cylinder 340 horsepower engine  that still today runs as smoothly as it  did when it was first born these days  you can buy one in perfect working order  for about 20 grand which is a nice  appreciation considering the fact that  they cost about five thousand when they  first came out but when you drive it  you'll feel way more posh than that the  two bench seats are smooth and plush the  steering wheel is as thin as a reed and  makes you feel like a real lady it's a  little different than how caddy is  branding itself today what with that  move toward a more aggressive edgy ride  but the desire to give drivers a strong  sense of style still remains the same  you
 

